Z||000000||Black87GT||12-20-2001||03:04 PM||||Tomorrow I gonna pick up my 87 GT fastback that I just bought.<P>Only thing is that the airco is empty because of a rebuild engine.  In Holland the R12 (old) airco has to be converted, because freon is not allowed anymore(thats why the airco has no freon anymore).<P>Has anyone experience with airco convertion?  Convertion of R12(CFK) to HFK(R134A) is that diffucult or expensive?<P>I also read something about putting R416A fluid into a R12 airco instead of freon Is this true?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||avengador1||12-20-2001||08:59 PM||||The previous owner of my Fiero had this conversion done. The air blows cold and seems to work fine. I would recomend doing it as R12 is harder to find now and a lot more expensive. I have seen do it yourself kits but I would only trust a professional to do it. The conversion was to R134.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||12-20-2001||09:51 PM||||I had the 134a conversion done to my 88.  It was about $125 US.  The conversion kits are cheaper to do yourself, but I was on vacation with the car whe the AC went and wanted it fixed fast.<P>It works ok, but doesn't seem as cold as the old R12.  The small Fiero cabin is an asset.||||reg||1
